Code and Code Accessories
User Stories
Objectives
The project should include a small but meaningful set of user stories.

User stories should:

Describe user-visible behavior of the app in a simple format, such as: "as a <role> I want to <action> so that I can <goal>"
Include acceptance criteria
Be small enough to complete during the project
Cover both essential and optional features
Be stored in docs/user_stories.md
Expected Quantity
5-10 user stories total
At least 3 essential stories
At least 1 sad-path/error-handling story
Assessment
User stories will be assessed based on whether:

There are enough stories for the size of the project
The stories describe real user-facing behavior
The stories are clear, testable, and relevant
The implemented app reflects the stories
Tests
Objectives
The project should include automated tests.  

The test suite should include:

Unit tests: low-level tests that verify specific functions or classes work correctly in isolation
Acceptance tests: high-level tests that verify the complete application satisfies end-user requirements
Tests for core/essential app behavior
Happy-path tests, where the user provides valid input
Sad-path tests, where the user provides invalid input or an error condition occurs
Tests that are fast, independent, repeatable, self-validating, and easy to run
A documented command for running the test suite
Expected Coverage
The test suite should cover the most important parts of the application.
A good target for statement coverage is at least 80%.
Regardless of coverage, there must be clear evidence that the core logic of the app is well-tested.
Use a coverage tool and include the coverage report or instructions to generate it in the documentation
Assessment
Tests will be assessed based on whether:

They run successfully.
They test meaningful behavior.
They include both happy and sad paths.
They are not overly-dependent on test order or shared state.
They cover the core application logic.
Code Quality
Objectives
The code should be clean, readable, and maintainable.

Code should:

Be organized into appropriate directories, files, classes, and modules
Avoid unnecessary duplication
Use meaningful names for variables, methods, classes, and files
Separate core logic from input/output where reasonable
Handle invalid user input gracefully
Avoid hard-coded values when constants or configuration would be more clear
Run without crashing during normal use
Have minimal (ideally zero) style offenses, e.g. as measured by RuboCop
Assessment
Code quality will be assessed based on:

Readability
Organization
Correctness
Error handling
Maintainability
Whether the app can be run successfully by the grader
Comments
Objectives
Comments should be used when they add useful context.

Good comments explain things which are not obvious from the code, e.g.

Why a decision was made
Important assumptions or limitations
Comments should not merely repeat what the code already says.

Assessment
Comments will be assessed based on whether:

Comments are up-to-date and useful
Comments explain non-obvious things
Commit Messages
Objectives
Commit messages should be clear, concise, and meaningful.

Good commit messages include:

A short subject line describing the change
Use the imperative mood when possible
A body for larger or non-obvious changes.
The reason for the change when appropriate.
Assessment
Commit messages will be assessed based on whether, in a sample of commits and code changes:

Commit messages clearly and accurately describe the changes
Commit messages are concise, but not overly so
Git
Objectives
Teams should use Git for collaborative development and version control.

Expected Git practices:

The project is stored in a GitHub repository
Every team member contributes to the repository
Commits are made regularly
The commit history shows incremental development
Large or last-minute commits should be avoided
Feature branches are encouraged
Pull requests are recommended for non-trivial changes
Assessment
Git usage will be assessed based on:

Contributions from all team members
Commit history demonstrates use of expected Git practices 
Pair Programming
Objectives
There should be evidence that the team practiced pair programming.

Expected pair programming practices:

Use driver/navigator roles
Switch roles regularly
Discuss design and implementation decisions together
Make sure both partners understand the code
Record evidence of pair-programming work in docs/pairing_log.md
